@meta
id: 08-20
title: Scenario — splitting the entity
chapter: 08
type: scenario
skills: delete-d, put-p, replace-r, insert-i
par: 88
minutes: 14
vscode: two tabs, four drags, two paste-into-the-wrong-place mistakes
open: order.entity.ts refund-runbook.md default.json
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-533  Order carries two fields that belong to Customer, and a
             legacy column that has not been written since the importer
             was deleted. Refunds are now a status in their own right.
             While you are in here, the runbook steps are in the order
             somebody thought of them, not the order you do them in.

Three files. `:bn` and `:bp` move between them, `:wqa` writes everything
and quits. Nothing here needs retyping: every line you need already
exists somewhere in the file.

  1. order.entity.ts

     - `customerEmail` and `customerName` move to `Customer`, directly
       under its `id`. Move them together; they are adjacent.
     - `legacyRef` goes.
     - `OrderStatus` needs `REFUNDED` as a fourth value, at the end of
       the union, before the semicolon.
     - `LegacyOrder` goes entirely, along with the blank line above it.

  2. refund-runbook.md

     The steps must read: check the window, get approval, raise the
     refund, note the reference, email the customer. Posting in
     `#payments` by hand is what the runbook is replacing, so that step
     goes. Renumber what is left so it runs 1 to 5.

  3. default.json — reference only. Change it and the check fails.

One hazard, and it is the whole chapter in one sentence: there is a
single register and every delete overwrites it. Carry one thing at a
time.

@keys
| `2dd` | delete two lines into the register |
| `p` `P` | put them below, or above |
| `6dd` | delete six lines |
| `r` | replace one character |
| `$` `i` | end of the line, then insert before that character |
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
In the entity, do the move before anything else. If you delete
`legacyRef` first, the two customer fields are gone from the register and
you will be retyping them.
--
The union needs `| 'REFUNDED'` inserted before the `;`, not appended
after it. `$` lands on the semicolon and `i` starts inserting to its
left.
--
In the runbook, move the lines first and renumber afterwards. Trying to
keep the numbers right while the lines are still moving is how you end up
with two step threes.
--
Renumbering is five `r` presses, bottom to top, with `k` between them.
The digit is the first character of each line, so the column never
changes.

@solution
**order.entity.ts**

    5j 2dd        take customerEmail and customerName
    6j p          put them under Customer's id
    7k dd         legacyRef
    2j $i | 'REFUNDED'<Esc>
    8j 6dd        the blank line and all of LegacyOrder

**refund-runbook.md** — `:bn`

    3j dd k P     the window check goes to the top
    3j dd 2k P    approval goes above the refund
    3j dd         the manual post goes
    r5 k r4 k r3 k r2 k r1

Then `:wqa`.

The entity half is one long lesson in register discipline. Four edits,
and exactly one of them is a move — so the move has to happen first,
undisturbed, before any of the deletes overwrite what it is carrying.
Experienced vim users do this without thinking about it, and the way they
learned was getting it wrong on something like this.

The runbook half is the opposite lesson: do the structural work first and
the cosmetic work last. The numbers are the least important thing on
those lines and the most tempting to keep tidy as you go. Move all the
lines, then walk up the list replacing digits — five two-key edits with
no thinking in them, because the hard part was already finished.

That is the shape of most real editing. Decide what goes where, move it
in as few operations as you can, then sweep once for the details.
Chapters 9 and 10 make each of those operations smaller. They do not
change the order you should do them in.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  customerEmail: string;
  customerName: string;
  legacyRef: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  createdAt: Date;
};

export type LegacyOrder = {
  id: string;
  ref: string;
  total: number;
};

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED' | 'REFUNDED';

export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  customerEmail: string;
  customerName: string;
  createdAt: Date;
};

@start refund-runbook.md
# Cancelling an order

1. Raise the refund in the gateway
2. Check the order is inside the 30 day window
3. Note the gateway reference on the order
4. Ask finance to approve anything over £500
5. Post the refund in #payments by hand
6. Email the customer

@expected refund-runbook.md
# Cancelling an order

1. Check the order is inside the 30 day window
2. Ask finance to approve anything over £500
3. Raise the refund in the gateway
4. Note the gateway reference on the order
5. Email the customer

